.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt FLOCK 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm flock
.Nd lock a whole file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/file.h
.Ft int
.Fn flock "int fd" "int operation"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn flock
sets or removes an advisory lock over the whole of what
.Fa fd
is open on.
.Fa operation
is
.Dv LOCK_SH
for a shared lock,
.Dv LOCK_EX
for an exclusive one, or
.Dv LOCK_UN
to remove the lock, with
.Dv LOCK_NB
added for no wait.
A lock is advisory: it stops another lock, never a read or a write.
.Pp
The lock belongs to the open-file description.
Descriptors made by
.Xr dup 2
and
.Xr fork 2
share it, so an unlock through any of them removes it, and it goes with
the description's last reference.
A second
.Xr open 2
of the file makes another description, whose lock is another owner's
even in the same process.
.Pp
An exclusive lock conflicts with every lock of another description, and
a shared one with an exclusive one.
A request that conflicts waits until the conflict is gone, or with
.Dv LOCK_NB
fails with
.Er EWOULDBLOCK .
A signal ends a wait with
.Er EINTR ,
which
.Dv SA_RESTART
restarts.
A request for the type the description already has succeeds at once; a
request for the other type first lets go of the lock the description
has, so a conversion is not atomic, and a failed one leaves no lock,
as on Linux.
A request that waits looks for the description's lock again when it
wakes, so threads that share a description and wait together end with
one lock.
.Pp
.Fn flock
locks and
.Xr fcntl 2
record locks do not conflict with each other.
A lock is on the file, found as
.Xr fcntl 2
finds a record lock's: it stays with the file through a rename, on KRFFS
also through a write that moves the file, and is not shared with another
KRFFS file whose name has the same hash; a descriptor of a pipe, a
socket, a device or a terminal is locked by the object it is open on.
.Dv LOCK_MAND ,
which Linux accepts and ignores, is accepted and ignored.
.Pp
The i386 system call number is 143.
The kernel takes the descriptor in EBX and the operation in ECX.
On x86-64 the number is 73, with the descriptor in RDI and the operation
in RSI.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn flock
returns 0 on success, or -1 on failure and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/musl/lockmusl.c
takes an exclusive lock on one open of a file, requires a second open's
.Dv LOCK_EX Ns | Ns Dv LOCK_NB
to fail with
.Er EWOULDBLOCK ,
removes the lock through a
.Xr dup 2
of the first descriptor, and checks that closing the second open's only
descriptor lets its lock go.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EWOULDBLOCK]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor, was opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
or a lock was asked for on one open neither for reading nor for writing.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal ended the wait.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa operation
is not one of the three.
.It Bq Er ENOLCK
The kernel heap has no room for the lock.
.It Bq Er EWOULDBLOCK
.Dv LOCK_NB
was given and another description's lock conflicts.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr close 2 ,
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr open 2
